Ladybird parses manifest.json in the UI process into one model for
manifest versions 2 and 3, reads the content scripts at load time,
and generates the page that hosts background scripts. WebKit’s
WebExtension::parseManifest() and its parse*Properties() helpers do
the same in the UI process, with generatedBackgroundContent() for
the background scripts and isWebAccessibleResource(requestURL,
pageURL) for resource access. Blink’s Extension::Create() runs the
manifest handlers (content_scripts_handler.cc, web_accessible_
resources_info.cc, csp_info.cc) in the browser process, generates
the background page in GenerateBackgroundPageContents() of
extension_protocols.cc, and gates resource loads with
AllowExtensionResourceLoad(). Gecko’s ExtensionData.parseManifest()
in Extension.sys.mjs produces the same shape, WebExtensionPolicy::
IsWebAccessiblePath() and SourceMayAccessPath() answer resource
access, and ExtensionProtocolHandler::AllowExternalResource() refuses
a file outside the extension directory, which is the check Ladybird’s
resolve_resource_path() makes segment by segment. The version-2
“permissions” list mixing host patterns with permission names is
split the way Gecko’s ExtensionData does in parseManifest().
